WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:04.386
Every access object the program
governs has a file in the repository,

2
00:00:04.386 --> 00:00:07.962
one per object, and the safety
rules travel with the files.

3
00:00:07.962 --> 00:00:12.540
Every blocking or grant policy
excludes the emergency access group,

4
00:00:12.540 --> 00:00:15.913
and new and changed policies
deploy as report-only.

5
00:00:15.913 --> 00:00:21.739
PIM settings require an authentication
context or MFA, justification, and approval

6
00:00:21.739 --> 00:00:27.150
for Tier 0, with no permanent active
assignment except the emergency accounts.

7
00:00:27.150 --> 00:00:30.737
Groups follow the naming
convention with no nested members.

8
00:00:30.737 --> 00:00:35.650
Packages have an expiry, and incompatible
packages are listed both ways.

9
00:00:35.650 --> 00:00:39.262
Review definitions have a
fallback reviewer and auto-apply.

10
00:00:39.262 --> 00:00:44.288
Leaver workflows remove all package
assignments and revoke refresh tokens.

11
00:00:44.288 --> 00:00:48.963
Four tools can hold those objects,
and every one ends in Microsoft Graph.

12
00:00:48.963 --> 00:00:51.831
Graph covers everything,
but holds no state,

13
00:00:51.831 --> 00:00:57.249
so desired state has to live in the repository
and the comparison has to be written.

14
00:00:57.249 --> 00:01:02.305
The Terraform azuread provider, at
version 3.10.0 as of October 2026,

15
00:01:02.305 --> 00:01:06.717
covers most classes but has
no access review definitions,

16
00:01:06.717 --> 00:01:12.875
no Lifecycle Workflows, and no role
management policy for Entra directory roles.

17
00:01:12.875 --> 00:01:20.000
Microsoft365DSC, an open-source community
module, covers every class in the table.

18
00:01:20.000 --> 00:01:24.345
And Microsoft Entra Tenant
Governance configuration management,

19
00:01:24.345 --> 00:01:26.984
built on generally available Graph APIs,

20
00:01:26.984 --> 00:01:31.562
monitors a baseline but doesn't cover
review definitions or workflows.

21
00:01:31.562 --> 00:01:34.250
The program's reference
choice minimizes write

22
00:01:34.250 --> 00:01:36.737
paths while keeping
drift detection native.

23
00:01:36.737 --> 00:01:42.467
Microsoft365DSC writes, because it's the
only declarative tool that covers every

24
00:01:42.467 --> 00:01:45.079
class; treat it as a community project,

25
00:01:45.079 --> 00:01:48.112
check its release notes,
and pin its version.

26
00:01:48.112 --> 00:01:51.866
A team already invested in Terraform
can use it where it covers,

27
00:01:51.866 --> 00:01:53.887
and Graph PowerShell for the rest.

28
00:01:53.887 --> 00:01:59.113
The rule that matters is one writer per
object class, recorded in the repository.

29
00:01:59.113 --> 00:02:02.412
The tenant monitor detects
drift where it covers;

30
00:02:02.412 --> 00:02:06.437
a daily read-only comparison
handles reviews and workflows.

31
00:02:06.437 --> 00:02:09.062
And Entra Exporter takes a weekly backup,

32
00:02:09.062 --> 00:02:12.670
kept separate from the
baselines, as Microsoft advises.
